   323|    <section class="article-hero">
   324|        <div class="article-hero-shell">
   325|            <span class="article-kicker">Dental treatment in China</span>
   326|            <h1>Dental Implant Cost in China (2026)</h1>
   327|            <p>Compare typical implant price ranges, treatment timelines, and clinics often considered by international patients before you decide whether China is the right fit.</p>
   328|            <div class="article-meta">
   329|                <span>Updated March 2026</span>
   330|                <span>8 min read</span>
   331|                <span>For international patients comparing treatment costs</span>
   332|            </div>
   333|        </div>
   334|    </section>
   335|
   336|    <main class="article-main">
   337|        <div class="article-shell">
   338|            <div class="article-grid">
   339|                <article class="article-content">
   340|                    <p class="article-intro"><strong>Dental implants in China</strong> are increasingly considered by international patients who want lower treatment costs without giving up access to major cities, private clinics, or internationally known implant systems. The key question is not only price. It is whether the clinic, implant brand, and treatment plan fit your case and travel timeline.</p>
   341|
   342|                    <div class="info-highlight">
   343|                        <h2>Quick cost snapshot</h2>
   344|                        <div class="price-line">From $800 per implant in China</div>
   345|                        <div class="savings-chip">Often 60 to 80% lower than typical US private pricing</div>
   346|                    </div>
   347|
   348|                    <h2>Why patients compare dental implant treatment in China</h2>
   349|                    <ul>
   350|                        <li><strong>Lower overall cost</strong>: often much lower than private pricing in the US, UK, or Australia.</li>
   351|                        <li><strong>Wide implant brand choice</strong>: premium and mid-range systems are commonly available.</li>
   352|                        <li><strong>Faster scheduling</strong>: many clinics can see international patients sooner than overloaded local systems.</li>
   353|                        <li><strong>Digital planning</strong>: CT scans, guided surgery, and restorative planning are common in higher-end clinics.</li>
   354|                        <li><strong>Large city options</strong>: Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Shenzhen all offer different clinic styles and price levels.</li>
   355|                    </ul>
   356|
   357|                    <h2>Dental implant costs in China vs the USA vs Australia</h2>
   358|                    <div class="cost-table-wrap">
   359|                        <table class="cost-table">
   360|                            <tr>
   361|                                <th>Procedure</th>
   362|                                <th>China</th>
   363|                                <th>USA</th>
   364|                                <th>Australia</th>
   365|                                <th>Typical Savings</th>
   366|                            </tr>
   367|                            <tr>
   368|                                <td>Single Dental Implant <span class="tag">Premium</span></td>
   369|                                <td>$800-$1,500</td>
   370|                                <td>$3,000-$5,000</td>
   371|                                <td>$2,500-$4,000</td>
   372|                                <td><strong>About 70%</strong></td>
   373|                            </tr>
   374|                            <tr>
   375|                                <td>Single Dental Implant <span class="tag">Standard</span></td>
   376|                                <td>$500-$800</td>
   377|                                <td>$2,000-$3,500</td>
   378|                                <td>$1,800-$3,000</td>
   379|                                <td><strong>About 70%</strong></td>
   380|                            </tr>
   381|                            <tr>
   382|                                <td>All-on-4 (Upper or Lower)</td>
   383|                                <td>$8,000-$15,000</td>
   384|                                <td>$25,000-$50,000</td>
   385|                                <td>$20,000-$40,000</td>
   386|                                <td><strong>About 65%</strong></td>
   387|                            </tr>
   388|                            <tr>
   389|                                <td>All-on-6 (Upper or Lower)</td>
   390|                                <td>$12,000-$18,000</td>
   391|                                <td>$30,000-$60,000</td>
   392|                                <td>$25,000-$50,000</td>
   393|                                <td><strong>About 65%</strong></td>
   394|                            </tr>
   395|                            <tr>
   396|                                <td>Full Mouth Reconstruction</td>
   397|                                <td>$15,000-$30,000</td>
   398|                                <td>$60,000-$120,000</td>
   399|                                <td>$50,000-$100,000</td>
   400|                                <td><strong>About 75%</strong></td>
   401|                            </tr>
   402|                            <tr>
   403|                                <td>Bone Grafting</td>
   404|                                <td>$200-$500</td>
   405|                                <td>$1,000-$3,000</td>
   406|                                <td>$800-$2,500</td>
   407|                                <td><strong>About 75%</strong></td>
   408|                            </tr>
   409|                            <tr>
   410|                                <td>Sinus Lift</td>
   411|                                <td>$300-$600</td>
   412|                                <td>$1,500-$4,000</td>
   413|                                <td>$1,200-$3,000</td>
   414|                                <td><strong>About 75%</strong></td>
   415|                            </tr>
   416|                        </table>
   417|                    </div>
   418|                    <p><em>These ranges are broad planning references. Final quotes vary by implant brand, bone condition, sedation needs, temporary prosthetics, and whether you are comparing a hospital department or a private clinic.</em></p>
   419|
   420|                    <h2>Common dental implant treatment types</h2>
   421|
   422|                    <h3>1. Single dental implant</h3>
   423|                    <p>Used when one missing tooth needs replacement. Usually includes the implant post, abutment, and crown, though some clinics quote these items separately.</p>
   424|
   425|                    <h3>2. All-on-4 dental implants</h3>
   426|                    <p>Full-arch restoration using four implants. Often chosen by patients with multiple missing teeth who want a lower implant count and faster provisional restoration.</p>
   427|
   428|                    <h3>3. All-on-6 dental implants</h3>
   429|                    <p>Full-arch restoration using six implants. This can provide added stability and may suit patients with different bone or bite considerations.</p>
   430|
   431|                    <h3>4. Zygomatic implants</h3>
   432|                    <p>Used in selected complex cases with severe upper-jaw bone loss. These are more specialized and should be reviewed carefully before travel.</p>
   433|
   434|                    <h3>5. Mini dental implants</h3>
   435|                    <p>Sometimes used in narrower ridges, temporary restorations, or specific clinical situations. They are not simply a cheaper version of standard implants and should be matched to the case.</p>
   436|
   437|                    <h2>Implant brands commonly available in China</h2>
   438|                    <p>Many clinics in China offer internationally recognized systems, including:</p>
   439|                    <ul>
   440|                        <li><strong>Premium systems</strong>: Nobel Biocare, Straumann, Astra Tech</li>
   441|                        <li><strong>Mid-range systems</strong>: Ankylos, Zimmer, Biomet 3i</li>
   442|                        <li><strong>Value-focused systems</strong>: Dentium, Osstem, MIS</li>
   443|                        <li><strong>Other systems</strong>: CAMLOG, BEGO, and others depending on clinic inventory and surgeon preference</li>
   444|                    </ul>
   445|
   446|                    <h2>Dental hospitals and clinics often considered by international patients</h2>
   447|                    <div class="hospital-list">
   448|                        <div class="hospital-card">
   449|                            <h4>Beijing United Family Hospital</h4>
   450|                            <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> international-standard private hospital dental center<br><strong>City:</strong> Beijing<br><strong>Notes:</strong> often considered by patients who want English-speaking support and a more familiar private-hospital environment.</p>
   451|                        </div>
   452|                        <div class="hospital-card">
   453|                            <h4>Shanghai Jiao Tong University Dental Hospital</h4>
   454|                            <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> major academic dental hospital<br><strong>City:</strong> Shanghai<br><strong>Notes:</strong> relevant for patients comparing large dental institutions and specialist depth.</p>
   455|                        </div>
   456|                        <div class="hospital-card">
   457|                            <h4>Guangzhou Dental Hospital</h4>
   458|                            <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> established dental hospital in South China<br><strong>City:</strong> Guangzhou<br><strong>Notes:</strong> often considered by patients comparing implant work with broader southern China treatment plans.</p>
   459|                        </div>
   460|                        <div class="hospital-card">
   461|                            <h4>Private digital dentistry clinics in Shenzhen</h4>
   462|                            <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> modern private clinic option<br><strong>City:</strong> Shenzhen<br><strong>Notes:</strong> can appeal to patients who prioritize private-clinic speed, digital workflows, and proximity to Hong Kong or the Pearl River Delta.</p>
   463|                        </div>
   464|                    </div>
   465|
   466|                    <h2>Your dental implant timeline in China</h2>
   467|
   468|                    <h3>Option 1: First-visit placement and temporary restoration</h3>
   469|                    <ol>
   470|                        <li><strong>Day 1</strong>: consultation, imaging, treatment planning</li>
   471|                        <li><strong>Day 2-3</strong>: implant surgery</li>
   472|                        <li><strong>Day 4-5</strong>: follow-up and temporary restoration where appropriate</li>
   473|                    </ol>
   474|                    <p><em>Permanent crowns often require healing time and may be completed later, either on a return visit or through a coordinated plan.</em></p>
   475|
   476|                    <h3>Option 2: Longer stay for more treatment steps</h3>
   477|                    <ol>
   478|                        <li><strong>Day 1-2</strong>: consultation, CT scan, extractions if needed</li>
   479|                        <li><strong>Day 3-5</strong>: implant placement and early healing</li>
   480|                        <li><strong>Day 6-10</strong>: abutment work or impressions depending on the case</li>
   481|                        <li><strong>Day 11-14</strong>: possible restorative steps for selected treatment plans</li>
   482|                    </ol>
   483|
   484|                    <h3>Option 3: All-on-4 or All-on-6 planning</h3>
   485|                    <p>Many patients receive a temporary prosthesis first and return later for the final prosthetic stage. This is why realistic travel planning matters as much as the headline price.</p>
   486|
   487|                    <div class="cta-box">
   488|                        <h3>Need help comparing dental options in China?</h3>
   489|                        <p>We can help you compare likely costs, shortlist hospitals or clinics, and figure out the next practical step before you travel.</p>
   490|                        <div class="cta-actions">
   491|                            <a href="../contact-new.html" class="cta-button">Start Free Case Review</a>
   492|                            <a href="../hospitals.html" class="cta-button alt">Browse Hospitals</a>
   493|                        </div>
   494|                    </div>
   495|
   496|                    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
   497|
   498|                    <h3>How long do dental implants usually last?</h3>
   499|                    <p>With good maintenance and a suitable treatment plan, implants can last many years. What matters most is not the country alone but bone quality, bite forces, hygiene, prosthetic design, and ongoing follow-up.</p>
