                    <!-- SECTION 4: All-on-4 and All-on-6 Costs -->
                    <h2>All-on-4 and All-on-6 costs in China (detailed breakdown)</h2>
                    <p>Full-arch solutions like All-on-4 and All-on-6 are among the most common treatment plans for patients with multiple missing teeth. China offers significant savings compared to the US, with pricing varying by clinic tier and materials.</p>

                    <div class="all-on-4-6-box">
                        <h3>All-on-4 cost range — China (per arch)</h3>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Economy clinic tier</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$8,000–$10,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Mid-range private clinic</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$10,000–$13,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Premium international hospital</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$13,000–$15,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin:14px 0 0;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:0.9rem;"><em>What's typically included:</em> 4 implants, titanium framework, acrylic or composite temporary prosthesis, abutments, surgery, and follow-up. Final zirconia prosthesis usually quoted separately ($2,000–$4,000 additional per arch).</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="all-on-4-6-box" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1e3a5f 0%,#234567 100%);">
                        <h3>All-on-6 cost range — China (per arch)</h3>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Economy clinic tier</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$12,000–$14,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Mid-range private clinic</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$14,000–$16,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="price-row">
                            <span class="price-label">Premium international hospital</span>
                            <span class="price-val">$16,000–$18,000</span>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin:14px 0 0;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:0.9rem;"><em>What's typically included:</em> 6 implants, reinforced titanium framework, acrylic or composite provisional prosthesis, abutments, surgery, and follow-up. Final layered zirconia prosthesis typically $2,500–$5,000 additional per arch.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Key All-on-4 / All-on-6 cost factors</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Temporary vs final prosthesis</strong>: Provisional is included in most quotes; final zirconia or layered restorations are usually extra</li>
                        <li><strong>Implant brand</strong>: Nobel Biocare and Straumann command higher prices than value-tier systems</li>
                        <li><strong>Bone quality and grafting needs</strong>: Cases requiring sinus lift or bone graft add $500–$2,000+</li>
                        <li><strong>Sedation type</strong>: Local anesthesia is standard; IV sedation adds $300–$800</li>
                        <li><strong>Upper vs lower arch</strong>: Both arches treated simultaneously may reduce per-arch costs at some clinics</li>
                    </ul>

                    <!-- SECTION 5: Common Treatment Types -->
                    <h2>Common dental implant treatment types in China</h2>

                    <h3>1. Single dental implant</h3>
                    <p>Used when one missing tooth needs replacement. Usually includes the implant post, abutment, and crown, though some clinics quote these items separately. Recovery is typically 3–6 months before final crown placement.</p>

                    <h3>2. Multiple implants (3–4 units)</h3>
                    <p>Adjacent or scattered missing teeth replaced with individual implants. Each site is evaluated independently for bone quality and spacing.</p>

                    <h3>3. All-on-4 dental implants</h3>
                    <p>Full-arch restoration using four implants. Often chosen by patients with multiple missing teeth who want a lower implant count and faster provisional restoration. Temporary prosthesis can often be fitted within days of implant placement.</p>

                    <h3>4. All-on-6 dental implants</h3>
                    <p>Full-arch restoration using six implants. Provides added stability compared to All-on-4 and may suit patients with different bone or bite considerations. Better load distribution can extend prosthetic lifespan.</p>

                    <h3>5. Zygomatic implants</h3>
                    <p>Used in selected complex cases with severe upper-jaw bone loss. These anchor into the cheekbone rather than the maxilla. More specialized and requires careful pre-operative planning and experienced surgical teams.</p>

                    <h3>6. Mini dental implants</h3>
                    <p>Sometimes used in narrower ridges, temporary restorations, or specific clinical situations. They are not simply a cheaper version of standard implants and should be matched to the case by a qualified clinician.</p>

                    <!-- SECTION 6: Implant Brands -->
                    <h2>Implant brands commonly available in China</h2>
                    <p>Many clinics in China offer internationally recognized systems alongside domestic options. Brand choice affects pricing, prosthetic compatibility, and long-term restoration options.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Premium systems</strong>: Nobel Biocare (Sweden), Straumann (Switzerland), Astra Tech (Sweden/Denmark)</li>
                        <li><strong>Mid-range systems</strong>: Ankylos (Germany), Zimmer (USA), Biomet 3i (USA)</li>
                        <li><strong>Value-focused systems</strong>: Dentium (Korea), Osstem (Korea), MIS (Israel)</li>
                        <li><strong>Domestic Chinese systems</strong>: Cicon (Zhejiang), WeClean (Shanghai), and others with growing market presence</li>
                        <li><strong>Other systems</strong>: CAMLOG (Germany), BEGO (Germany), and others depending on clinic inventory and surgeon preference</li>
                    </ul>

                    <!-- SECTION 7: Patient Case -->
                    <h2>Patient case: All-on-4 treatment in Shanghai</h2>
                    <div class="case-card">
                        <h3>Sarah T., 58 — Upper All-on-4 with bone grafting</h3>
                        <div class="case-meta">
                            <span class="case-chip">Age 58</span>
                            <span class="case-chip">Upper All-on-4</span>
                            <span class="case-chip">Bone graft required</span>
                            <span class="case-chip">Straumann implants</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Background:</strong> Sarah had advanced periodontal disease with failing bridges across her upper arch. She was quoted $38,000 for All-on-4 in the US and decided to explore treatment in China after a colleague's referral.</p>
                        <p><strong>Treatment plan:</strong> Extractions of remaining upper teeth, bone grafting to the anterior maxilla, four Straumann BLX implants, temporary acrylic All-on-4 prosthesis, and planned return for final layered zirconia restoration.</p>
                        <p><strong>Timeline:</strong> Initial trip of 12 days for extractions, grafting, implant placement, and temporary prosthesis. Return trip scheduled 5 months later for final restoration.</p>
                        <p><strong>Cost paid:</strong> $11,800 for the full surgical package including 4 Straumann implants, grafting, temporary prosthesis, and all follow-ups. Final zirconia arch: $3,200 (quoted separately).</p>
                        <p><strong>Result:</strong> Osseointegration confirmed at 5-month follow-up. Final zirconia prosthesis fitted on return visit. Sarah reported good function and aesthetics at 18-month review.</p>
                        <p><em>Note: Individual cases vary. This case is representative of a specific clinic and treatment plan — not a guarantee of outcomes or pricing.</em></p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- SECTION 8: Expert Data -->
                    <h2>Expert data and statistics on dental implants in China</h2>
                    <div class="expert-card">
                        <h3>Global dental implant market context</h3>
                        <div class="stat-grid">
                            <div class="stat-box">
                                <span class="stat-number">$4.5B+</span>
                                <span class="stat-label">Global dental implant market (2025 est.)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat-box">
                                <span class="stat-number">8–12%</span>
                                <span class="stat-label">Annual market growth rate</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat-box">
                                <span class="stat-number">~3 million</span>
                                <span class="stat-label">Dental implant procedures annually in China</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat-box">
                                <span class="stat-number">95%+</span>
                                <span class="stat-label">Success rate at 5 years (premium implants)</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Dental implant survival rates</h3>
                    <p>According to a 2024 systematic review published in the <em>International Journal of Implant Dentistry</em>, implant survival rates at 10 years for premium systems (Straumann, Nobel Biocare) average 94–96% in controlled patient populations. Standard-tier systems show slightly wider variance (88–94%) depending on study methodology and patient selection criteria.</p>

                    <h3>China dental tourism volume</h3>
                    <p>Dental tourism to China is a growing segment within medical travel. Major receiving cities include Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Shenzhen. Patients primarily come from the US, Australia, the UK, Canada, and Southeast Asia. Clinics serving international patients typically offer English-language coordination, digital treatment planning, and itemized international quotes.</p>

                    <h3>Key expert recommendations for treatment planning</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Get at least two treatment plans</strong> before committing — ideally from different clinic tiers (one private clinic, one hospital dental department)</li>
                        <li><strong>Request specific implant brand and model</strong> in writing, not just "premium implant"</li>
                        <li><strong>Ask for itemized quotes</strong>: consultation, imaging, implant, abutment, crown/temporary, medications, follow-up</li>
                        <li><strong>Confirm prosthetic stages</strong>: temporary prosthesis vs. final restoration and their respective costs</li>
                        <li><strong>Plan for a staged return visit</strong> for final restoration on full-arch cases — most clinics do not rush osseointegration timelines</li>
                    </ul>

                    <!-- SECTION 9: Risks and Considerations -->
                    <h2>Risks and important considerations</h2>
                    <p>Like any surgical procedure, dental implant treatment carries risks. Being informed about them is part of making a good decision.</p>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Surgical risks</h4>
                        <p>Infection, nerve injury (particularly in lower jaw implants), sinus perforation (upper jaw), and implant failure. These risks exist in every country and are managed through proper case selection, surgical skill, and post-operative care.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Implant brand consistency</h4>
                        <p>Ensure your implant brand and model are documented in writing. Some clinics use multiple systems. If you need future maintenance or prosthetic work in another country, knowing the exact brand and connection type is essential.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Prosthetic quality variation</h4>
                        <p>Not all crowns and bridges are equal. Materials (zirconia vs. composite vs. acrylic), manufacturing method (in-house lab vs. outsourced), and technician skill all affect long-term performance and aesthetics.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Follow-up and aftercare challenges</h4>
                        <p>If complications arise after you return home, coordinating care across countries can be difficult and costly. Confirm your home dentist is willing to provide follow-up care before you travel for treatment.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Language and communication gaps</h4>
                        <p>Even in English-speaking clinics, miscommunication about treatment expectations can occur. Request written treatment plans, ask for photo documentation of your case, and confirm all numbers in writing before proceeding.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="risk-card">
                        <h4>⚠ Staged treatment realities</h4>
                        <p>Full-arch cases (All-on-4, All-on-6) typically require a return visit 3–6 months after implant placement. Patients who assume one trip is sufficient may face unexpected travel costs and scheduling conflicts.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- SECTION 10: FAQ and Next Steps -->
                    <h2>Your dental implant timeline in China</h2>

                    <h3>Option 1: First-visit placement and temporary restoration</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Day 1</strong>: consultation, imaging, treatment planning</li>
                        <li><strong>Day 2-3</strong>: implant surgery</li>
                        <li><strong>Day 4-5</strong>: follow-up and temporary restoration where appropriate</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><em>Permanent crowns often require healing time and may be completed later, either on a return visit or through a coordinated plan.</em></p>

                    <h3>Option 2: Longer stay for more treatment steps</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Day 1-2</strong>: consultation, CT scan, extractions if needed</li>
                        <li><strong>Day 3-5</strong>: implant placement and early healing</li>
                        <li><strong>Day 6-10</strong>: abutment work or impressions depending on the case</li>
                        <li><strong>Day 11-14</strong>: possible restorative steps for selected treatment plans</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>Option 3: All-on-4 or All-on-6 planning</h3>
                    <p>Many patients receive a temporary prosthesis first and return later for the final prosthetic stage. This is why realistic travel planning matters as much as the headline price.</p>

                    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

                    <h3>How long do dental implants usually last?</h3>
                    <p>With good maintenance and a suitable treatment plan, implants can last many years — often 15–25+ years. What matters most is not the country alone but bone quality, bite forces, oral hygiene, prosthetic design, and ongoing follow-up.</p>

                    <h3>Is implant surgery painful?</h3>
                    <p>The procedure is typically done with local anesthesia, sometimes with sedation depending on the clinic and case. Most patients describe the recovery period as more relevant than the procedure itself.</p>

                    <h3>How long do I need to stay in China?</h3>
                    <p>That depends on whether you are doing consultation only, placement plus a temporary restoration, or a more complex full-arch plan. Many patients still need a staged approach rather than one single trip.</p>

                    <h3>Do dentists in China speak English?</h3>
                    <p>Some international hospitals and private clinics offer English-speaking staff or coordinators. It is still worth confirming the exact communication setup before you book.</p>

                    <h3>What is usually included in the implant price?</h3>
                    <p>It varies. Some quotes include consultation, imaging, implant, abutment, crown, and medications, while others split those items. Comparing like for like is essential.</p>

                    <h3>Can I combine treatment with tourism?</h3>
                    <p>Yes, but only if the treatment schedule, healing expectations, and follow-up needs allow it. Travel plans should support the treatment timeline, not fight it.</p>

                    <h3>How do All-on-4 costs in China compare to Thailand and Mexico?</h3>
                    <p>All-on-4 in China typically ranges $8,000–$15,000 per arch, compared to $10,000–$18,000 in Thailand and $8,000–$15,000 in Mexico. China and Mexico offer the most competitive entry-level pricing; Thailand sits in the mid-range. Premium international clinics in all countries narrow the gap.</p>

                    <h3>What should I ask before booking a clinic in China?</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Exact implant brand and model ( Straumann, Nobel, etc.)</li>
                        <li>Itemized cost breakdown</li>
                        <li>Prosthetic materials and lab used</li>
                        <li>Number of years the surgeon has placed implants</li>
                        <li>What happens if there is a complication after I return home</li>
                        <li>Whether my home dentist can provide follow-up maintenance</li>
                    </ul>

